<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/neurotransmitters-and-drugs">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Neurotransmitters are <strong>the brain's chemical vocabulary</strong> — and psychoactive
    drugs (caffeine to antipsychotics) are words injected into the conversation. Each
    transmitter system modulates a different aspect of state: wanting, mood, alertness,
    inhibition. Understanding them is understanding both psychiatry's pharmacology and everyday
    chemistry (coffee, alcohol, nicotine).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the major systems and how drugs plug into them" widget="equationStep" config={neurotransCfg} />

  <h2>The drug classes, mapped to circuits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stimulants (caffeine, cocaine, amphetamine):</strong> raise dopamine and norepinephrine signalling — alertness, focus, and (for the illegal ones) addiction risk in proportion to the spike.</li>
    <li><strong>Depressants (alcohol, benzodiazepines):</strong> enhance GABA inhibition — anxiolytic at low doses, sedating at higher; the withdrawal (opposite of effect) is what makes them dangerous to quit abruptly.</li>
    <li><strong>Hallucinogens (psilocybin, LSD):</strong> serotonin 2A receptor agonists — causing cortical flexibility; renewed clinical trials for depression show promise under controlled conditions (Oregon legalized supervised use, 2020).</li>
    <li><strong>Opioids:</strong> mimic endorphins at opioid receptors — unmatched pain relief, unmatched dependence risk. The overdose crisis (fentanyl's potency) is receptor pharmacology with catastrophic stakes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Berridge & Robinson on wanting vs liking; Nestler et al., Molecular Neuropharmacology; Nutt, Drugs Without the Hot Air.</p>
</TopicPage>
